  "historical_output_text": " \n\nOne of the most fundamental biological processes is photosynthesis, which plants use to convert light energy into chemical energy to fuel their growth and survival. At its core, photosynthesis harnesses sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose and oxygen. \n\nThe process occurs in the chloroplasts, specialized organelles in plant cells. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that absorbs light energy. In the first major step, called light-dependent reactions, chlorophyll captures light energy and uses it to generate ATP (adenosine triphosphate) and NADPH (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate). These energy-rich molecules are then used in the second step, the Calvin cycle, to convert carbon dioxide into glucose through a series of enzymatic reactions. Oxygen, a byproduct, is released into the",
